@Veritasium 
Can you redo the laser experiment with higher quality?
Many suspect that the scattering of a laser source like the one you used could distort the result. There are those here who propose to close the beam in a tube long enough to contain the photons that deviate from the main trajectory but it probably would not be enough, even the slightest deviation of the beam would hit the microscopic imperfections of the edge of the tube making some photons deviate in random directions (therefore also on the plane).

@Veritasium
You should do an experiment that demonstrates that the amount of light on the grid film is greater, or totally disconnected, from that given by the scattering.

@veritasium Your channel is the latest among several channels I subscribe to to get a "community feed" block constantly pushed into my YT feed. The posts are mostly unrelated to the channels they purport to be a part of and seem like a lot of AI bot chatter. I feel like this is some way to make it appear that YT is getting more engagement than it is, but even if the posts are being made by legitimate humans, it seems like it would only give a platform to spammers and trolls. There certainly doesn't seem to be much discussion of science education on there.

General consensus seems to be that this is a terrible feature that people don't want. I know this is easy to scroll past, but I do think it's harmful to channels' brands and users in general. I've been unsubscribing to any channels that implement this feature.

It occurs to me that maybe you didn't have a choice in the matter or aren't even aware of it. I would certainly like to remain subscribed to Veritasium. I'm hoping that posting here may prompt you to comment your thoughts on the matter, and possibly take action to remove the feature or allow users to opt in or out.

